.TH CCHAN_TIMED_RECV 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cchan_timed_recv \- receive a message with a timeout
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t cchan_timed_recv(cchan *ch,
                               cmessage_t *target_buf,
                               uint64_t timeout_us);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Works like cchan_recv(), but waits for a message for at most
timeout_us microseconds. The queue is the one that the channel picks
for the calling thread.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ch          Channel to receive from
target_buf  Buffer that gets the message
timeout_us  Longest time to wait, in microseconds, from the call. 0
            does not wait, which makes the call cchan_try_recv.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success              - A message was received
ccol_invalid_args         - ch or target_buf is NULL
ccol_container_empty      - The timeout is 0 and the chosen queue is
                            empty
ccol_timed_out            - The timeout ended
ccol_unexpected_failure   - The wait failed with an error other than a
                            timeout; errno holds that error
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The call never blocks for longer than the timeout. The direction is
decided from the ID of the calling thread.

With a timeout of 0 the call doesn't wait at all. It does exactly what
cchan_try_recv does and returns that result, so an empty queue
gives ccol_container_empty and not ccol_timed_out.

A timeout too large to form a deadline, such as UINT64_MAX, saturates
to the latest deadline the clock can hold, which in practice means
waiting with no end. It never wraps around into the past.

The timeout is measured on CLOCK_MONOTONIC. Changes to the system's
wall clock (made by an administrator, by NTP, or by resuming a virtual
machine) don't make it longer or shorter, and the call waits for the
duration that the caller gave.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cchan_recv (3),
.BR cchan_try_recv (3)
